{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>It is not good that the man should be alone.</b> Until now, concerning each one of them, He mentioned 'that it was good,' because the good is existence in all species; but in the creation of man, since the species cannot be sustained through him alone, He said 'not good.' And the explanation of the verse is: it is not good for the man to be in a single combination of two faces (דו פרצופין), and this is what they said — 'two faces were created' — and this is what is written: ״זכר ונקבה בראם״, and they were made in that single combination in such a manner that within them was a nature bringing forth in the organs of generation, a power begetting from the male to the female, and the second face was a helper to the first in its generation; therefore He said: 'I will make for him a helper opposite him' — it is good that he be separated from it and joined to it according to his will, and each one stand as one and one by itself, and he have a helper standing opposite him. And according to this there would be in the matter a retraction and a change of will, and far be it from El to do this." "<b>But</b> the essence of the verse 'it is not good that the man should be alone' was not said after the creation of man, but rather at the beginning, before the creation: when it arose in thought to create, the Holy One, blessed be He, said 'it is not good that the man should be alone' — that he should be solitary in the lower world, percipient, standing, and enduring forever like the ministering angels; 'I will make for him a helper opposite him' — that man should be a species, his individuals multiplying one from another, coming into being and passing away like the rest of the living creatures." "<b>And from this</b> he proceeds to clarify the creation of the woman, that she was taken from his ribs, and there is no retraction or change of will, far be it, in the matter, because the verse was said before the creation of man. And what the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted — said Rabbi Abbahu: it is written ״זכר ונקבה בראם״ and it is written ״ברא אותו״ — rather, at first it arose in thought to create two, and in the end only one was created; and behold we see that in the end two were created when Eve was taken from man — and from this the matter of retraction and change of will appears." "<b>And my master and teacher</b> R. Solomon, of blessed memory, wrote: one may interpret here two matters, and both are true in my opinion. What Rabbi Abbahu said, 'at first it arose in thought to create two' — know that the words of Scripture and the Aggadot are allusions and material depictions for the conceptualization of matters in the soul, and in order to awaken that all was created with providence from Him, blessed be He, toward the perfection of completion, He likened the matters to a matter arrived at through thought. And he said: the creation of man was in thought and contemplation, and it arose in thought to create two — that is, this one for itself and that one for itself, and they would stand independently in order that one receive from the other, for the form of male and female is like the matter of the sun and the moon; and afterward wisdom required that it is not good for man, who is the principal of creation, to be alone, but rather that he be the active agent and the female as a vessel, like the matter of the sun and the moon, in which the moon is nothing but as a vessel and the sun acts upon it and it receives from it; and this is the statement of Rabbi Abbahu: 'at first it arose in thought to create two' — this one for itself and that one for itself — 'and in the end, in deed, only one was created,' namely the male; and even though the female was taken from him and they were two, the female was not reckoned in the creation, for she is only as something appended to the principal, taken from him for the purpose of his use, and therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, called her 'a tail.'" "<b>Furthermore,</b> one may interpret: 'at first it arose in thought to create two,' like the manner of creation of the rest of the living creatures — the male by itself and the female by itself — 'and in the end only one was created,' the male alone, in order that the female be taken from his ribs, to be uniquely designated for his use like one of his organs uniquely designated for his service, and he too would strive for her benefit as he would strive for the benefit of his own body. Thus far the words of our master and teacher, of blessed memory."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:32.933468874Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}